 for bestpractices and # Run History

## Overview
Create the Run History experience for LifeOps: a searchable, paginated index of all workflow and cronjob runs. The page presents a compact summary for each run (status, duration, cronjob/workflow, owner, timestamp) with quick access to run details. It connects to global search and advanced filters, supports efficient data retrieval via caching, and serves as the gateway to the Run Detail view. This module leverages the Multi-Agent Orchestration Layer and Cronjobs Engine to provide rich run metadata, artifacts, and inter-agent traces. All actions are auditable, reversible where applicable, and designed for low-latency interactions in a dark-mode-first UI.

## Page Description (Full Detail)
What this page is:
- A centralized, searchable index of every run generated by workflows and cronjobs managed by LifeOps. Each row displays a concise summary: run ID, cronjob/workflow name, status (success, failed, running, canceled, pending), duration, start time, and owner. The queue includes quick actions and shortcuts to detailed investigations.

Goals:
- Allow users to quickly locate runs using a robust global search and multi-parameter filters (content, runs, cronjobs, projects, transactions).
- Provide status indicators, duration metrics, and contextual metadata (owner, cronjob, workflow, environment, time window).
- Offer quick access to per-run details (Run Detail) and bulk actions (re-run, export logs).
- Support performance optimizations and caching to minimize latency and cost associated with LLM-driven trace data.

Connected features:
- Search & Filter: Global search across runs, cronjobs, projects, transactions with advanced filters (date-range, status, owner, environment, duration thresholds, tags).
- Performance & Caching: Cache frequently accessed run lists and summaries, implement client-side data normalization, and use memoization strategies to reduce repeated computations.
- Multi-Agent Orchestration Layer: Reflects inter-agent traces, handoffs, and consensus outcomes in run details; supports alerting and traceability for run steps.
- Cronjobs Engine: Treats cronjobs as first-class objects; the Run History presents per-run outcomes including next/last run times, inputs, outputs, and aggregated run artifacts.

UI elements and guidance:
- Run table with columns: Run ID (linkable), Cronjob/Workflow name (with icon), Status badge, Duration (ms or human-friendly), Start Time, Owner, Environment, Tags, and Quick Actions.
- State indicators: Colorful status badges (green for success, red for failure, amber for running/pending, gray for canceled).
- Filters bar: Global search box, per-column filters (status, owner, cronjob, project, environment), date range picker, and duration slider.
- Bulk actions: Checkboxes for multi-select, actions like Re-run, Export Logs (CSV/JSON), Archive, Delete (with caution prompts).
- Run Detail quick link: Each row or row action navigates to Run Detail page.
- Pagination: Server-driven or cursor-based pagination with page controls and per-page limits.
- Keyboard accessibility: Focus traps, accessible labels, and ARIA attributes for status and actions.

Integration and API considerations:
- No external APIs required beyond LifeOps internal APIs; all run data originates from the orchestration layer, cronjobs engine, and run artifacts store.
- The Run History page should reflect live data with a refresh mechanism and real-time indicators where applicable (e.g., ongoing runs).
- Ensure error boundaries and graceful empty states when no runs exist or filters yield no results.

## Components to Build
- RunHistoryPage
  - Layout: 12-column grid with left-hand sidebar navigation and a main content area.
  - Subcomponents:
    - RunTable: Renders a list of RunRow items; supports selection and bulk actions.
    - RunRow: Displays run summary; includes status badge, duration, start time, owner, cronjob/workflow name, environment, and quick actions.
    - RunDetailLink: Navigates to Run Detail page with proper routing state.
    - FiltersBar: Global search, status filter, owner filter, cronjob/workflow filter, project filter, environment filter, date range, and duration filters.
    - PaginationBar: Page controls, per-page selector, and total count.
    - BulkActionsPanel: Sticky/floating panel for bulk actions when rows are selected.
  - Data handling:
    - Fetch runs with support for filters, search terms, and pagination.
    - Normalize and guard against null values: use data ?? [], Array.isArray checks, and safe mapping.
- RunDetailPage
  - Layout: Two-column or card-based sections showing inputs, outputs, inter-agent trace, logs, artifacts, diffs, errors, and reversible actions if applicable.
  - Subcomponents:
    - TraceViewer: Visualizes inter-agent messages with a trace timeline (or collapsible steps).
    - ArtifactsPanel: List and preview artifacts with download links.
    - LogsPanel: Log viewer with streaming or paged logs.
    - DiffsPanel: Display diffs between inputs/outputs and previous states.
    - RevertActionsPanel: Reversible actions with confirmation prompts and undo history.
  - Data handling:
    - Fetch run detail by run ID; guard against missing or nullable fields.
- SearchBar
  - Unified search input with suggestions and keyboard shortcuts.
- StatusBadge
  - Reusable badge with color mappings for statuses.

## Implementation Requirements

### Frontend
- Tech stack guidance:
  - React with TypeScript
  - State management: React Context or lightweight state, with useState/useEffect; consider SWR or React Query for data fetching and caching.
  - Styling: Theming consistent with the Visual Style; CSS-in-JS (e.g., styled-components) or CSS Modules as per project conventions.
- Data handling:
  - All arrays must be guarded: (data ?? []).map(...) or Array.isArray(data) ? data.map(...) : [].
  - Use useState<T[]>([]) for lists; useState<{ [key: string]: any } | null>(null) only when appropriate; prefer explicit default shapes.
  - Normalize API responses: const runs = Array.isArray(response?.data?.runs) ? response.data.runs : [].
  - Use optional chaining for nested objects: obj?.property?.nested.
- UI behavior:
  - Debounced global search to avoid excessive queries.
  - Client-side caching for the current page and recently viewed runs.
  - Loading skeletons for table and run detail to improve perceived performance.
  - Accessible components with proper aria-labels, roles, and keyboard navigation.
  - Keyboard shortcuts: / to focus search, g then r to go to Run History, etc. (configurable).
- Performance:
  - Implement data fetching with pagination and cursor-based approach where possible.
  - Cache frequent filters; invalidate cache on CRUD operations (create/run, update, delete).
  - Lazy load Run Detail sections; fetch heavy artifacts on demand.
- Security/UI integrity:
  - Ensure only authorized users can view runs; hide sensitive fields as needed.
  - Validate user inputs on the client before sending to API.

### Backend
- APIs:
  - Runs API
    - GET /runs
      - Query params: search, status[], owner[], cronjobId[], workflowId[], projectId[], environment, startDate, endDate, minDuration, maxDuration, page, pageSize, sort
      - Response: { data: Run[], total: number, page: number, pageSize: number }
    - GET /runs/{id}
      - Response: RunDetail including inputs, outputs, trace, logs, artifacts, diffs, errors, canRevert
    - POST /runs/{id}/rerun
      - Triggers a rerun; returns updated Run
    - GET /runs/{id}/logs
      - Streaming or paged logs
  - RunDetail API
    - GET /runs/{id}/detail
      - Payload with inter-agent trace, artifacts, diffs
  - Bulk actions
    - POST /runs/bulk
      - Body: { action: "rerun" | "export", runIds: string[] }
- Database schema (core ideas):
  - Runs
    - id (string)
    - cronjobId or workflowId (string | null)
    - ownerId (string)
    - status (enum: pending, running, success, failed, canceled)
    - startTime (timestamp)
    - endTime (timestamp)
    - durationMs (number)
    - environment (string)
    - inputSnapshot (JSON)
    - outputSnapshot (JSON)
    - trace (JSON) // inter-agent messages
    - logs (JSON or separate Logs table)
    - artifacts (JSON)
    - diffs (JSON)
    - errors (JSON)
    - reversible (boolean)
    - createdAt, updatedAt
  - RunArtifacts, RunLogs, RunDiffs as separate tables if large
- Validation:
  - Validate all inputs server-side; sanitize inputs to prevent injections.
  - Ensure response shapes are consistent; never return undefined arrays.
- Caching:
  - Implement server-side cache for frequent /runs queries with appropriate TTLs.
  - Consider per-user or per-filter cache keys.
- Security:
  - Authentication (JWT or session) and authorization checks on all endpoints.
  - Ensure users only access runs they own or have rights to view.

### Integration
- Data flow:
  - Run History fetches data from Runs API; Run Detail fetches data from Run detail endpoints.
  - When a run is rerun or logs are requested, the orchestration layer handles the action, and Run History updates accordingly.
- Inter-module communication:
  - Run History should honor trace data from the Multi-Agent Orchestration Layer and Cronjobs Engine; display inter-agent traces in Run Detail.
- Caching strategy:
  - Client caches: recent lists, current filters, and expanded run details for quick re-open.
  - Server caches: runs list per filter/page, and frequently requested run detail payloads.

## User Experience Flow
1) Landing and Global Search
- User lands on Run History page; see a prominent search bar and filters bar.
- Type in a keyword or use autocomplete suggestions to narrow results.

2) Filter and Navigate
- User applies status, cronjob/workflow, owner, environment, date range, and duration filters.
- Results update with smooth transitions; skeletons show while loading.

3) Explore Runs
- User scrolls through RunTable; each row shows concise info and a status badge.
- User can select one or more runs to perform bulk actions (rerun, export logs, etc.).

4) View Run Detail
- User clicks a Run Detail link or row action; Run Detail page opens.
- Run Detail shows inputs, outputs, trace, logs, artifacts, diffs, errors, and reversible actions if available.
- Heavy artifacts load on demand; user can review, then revert or re-run as needed.

5) Actions
- User triggers rerun; frontend sends request to /runs/{id}/rerun; Run History refreshes the affected run's status and meters.

6) Performance and Caching
- Repeated views of the same filter/page are served from cache; users experience near-instant responses.

## Technical Specifications

Data Models: [Schema details]
- Runs
  - id: string
  - cronjobId?: string
  - workflowId?: string
  - ownerId: string
  - status: "pending" | "running" | "success" | "failed" | "canceled"
  - startTime: string (ISO)
  - endTime?: string (ISO)
  - durationMs?: number
  - environment: string
  - inputSnapshot: any
  - outputSnapshot: any
  - trace: any
  - logs?: any
  - artifacts?: any
  - diffs?: any
  - errors?: any
  - reversible?: boolean
  - createdAt: string
  - updatedAt: string
- RunLogs
  - id
  - runId
  - timestamp
  - level
  - message
  - meta
- RunArtifacts
  - id
  - runId
  - name
  - type
  - url
  - preview
- RunDiffs
  - id
  - runId
  - path
  - diff

API Endpoints: [Routes and methods]
- GET /runs
  - Query: search, status[], ownerId, cronjobId, workflowId, projectId, environment, startDate, endDate, minDuration, maxDuration, page, pageSize, sort
  - Response: { data: Run[], total: number, page: number, pageSize: number }
- GET /runs/{id}
  - Response: Run with summary fields
- GET /runs/{id}/detail
  - Response: RunDetailPayload { inputs, outputs, trace, logs, artifacts, diffs, errors, reversible }
- POST /runs/{id}/rerun
  - Response: Updated Run
- GET /runs/{id}/logs
  - Response: Logs or streaming
- POST /runs/bulk
  - Body: { action: "rerun" | "export", runIds: string[] }

Security: [Authentication, authorization requirements]
- Require authentication for all Run History endpoints.
- Authorization: users can view runs within their projects/teams; restricted data exposure based on role.
- Input validation and output schema validation on every endpoint.
- Sensitive fields must be redacted or omitted per policy.

Validation: [Input validation rules]
- validate query params types and ranges
- ensure arrays are sanitized and deduplicated
- ensure runIds exist and belong to user on bulk actions

Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Run History loads with no runtime errors; data shapes match API specs; (data ?? []) guards are in place for lists.
- [ ] All array operations guard against null/undefined; components render with proper defaults and no crashes when data is missing.
- [ ] Global search, date-range filters, and status filters function correctly; results reflect accurate paging and totals.
- [ ] Run Detail page shows inputs, outputs, trace, logs, artifacts, diffs, errors; heavy sections load on demand.
- [ ] Bulk actions (rerun, export logs) perform correctly and reflect in subsequent lists.
- [ ] Performance: initial load under baseline latency target; subsequent navigations utilize client/server caching appropriately.
- [ ] Accessibility: ARIA labels, keyboard navigation, and focus management implemented.

UI/UX Guidelines
Apply the project's design system:
- Dark-mode-first, high-contrast UI with flat blocks and subtle elevation.
- 12-column grid with generous spacing (24–32px gutters/padding).
- Cards: 12–16px radius, subtle shadows; hover states.
- Navigation: Sidebar with icons, active states highlighted; top bar with search.
- Data Visualization: If used, charts align with neon accents on dark background.
- Interactive elements: Primary actions in blue (#4F8CFF); status indicators in green/yellow/red; inputs with soft borders and accessible focus outlines.
- Typography: Inter-like sans-serif; bold headings, semi-bold section titles, regular body text.
- Accessibility and keyboard support: clear focus states, semantic structure, descriptive labels.

Mandatory Coding Standards — Runtime Safety
CRITICAL: Follow these rules in ALL generated code to prevent runtime crashes.

1) Supabase / API results:
- Use nullish coalescing: const items = data ?? [].
- When mapping: (data ?? []).map(...) or Array.isArray(data) ? data.map(...) : [].

2) Array methods:
- Guard all array calls: (items ?? []).map(...), Array.isArray(items) ? items.filter(...) : [].

3) useState defaults for arrays/objects:
- Initialize arrays: const [list, setList] = useState<Type[]>([]).

4) API response shapes:
- const runs = Array.isArray(response?.data?.runs) ? response.data.runs : [].

5) Optional chaining:
- Use safe access: obj?.property?.nested.

6) Destructuring with defaults:
- const { items = [], count = 0 } = response ?? {};

Connected Pages:
- Run Detail: Full details of a single run: inputs, outputs, inter-agent trace, logs, artifacts, diffs, errors, and revert actions (if reversible).

Connected Features:
- Search & Filter, Performance & Caching, Multi-Agent Orchestration Layer, Cronjobs Engine.

Generated artifacts:
- Provide a single cohesive, production-ready prompt that can be fed to an AI development tool, including explicit APIs, data models, UI components, and integration points, while enforcing runtime safety rules.

Notes for the AI developer tool:
- Ensure all data-fetching hooks guard against nulls and provide loading/error states.
- Use TypeScript types/interfaces for Run, RunDetail, etc.
- Document API contracts clearly in the prompt so the backend can implement or mock them.
- Include test cases ideas (unit/integration) to verify runtime safety rules (nullish guards, array checks, etc.).
- Do not assume external API availability; design with mock endpoints and clear data shapes. for style.

CRITICAL DESIGN REQUIREMENTS:
- You MUST follow ALL design principles and patterns specified in the Design Reference above
- Do NOT deviate from the design specifications without explicit user permission
- Implement ALL visual design elements (colors, typography, spacing, shadows, animations) as specified
- Follow the layout patterns and component designs exactly as documented
- Ensure all interactions and micro-animations match the reference specifications

CRITICAL TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS:
- React 18.3.1, React Router 6.30.1
- Tailwind CSS v3 with tailwind.config.js and custom keyframes
- @tailwindcss/typography plugin for rich text content
- Tailwind CSS animations (NOT Motion library or framer-motion)
- Sonner for toasts
- Recharts for data visualization
- Native fetch() with API utilities in src/lib/api.ts
- Design system with CSS custom properties
- Use RGB color values for theming
- When Supabase is configured: use Edge Functions for LLM and server-only logic; never expose API keys in the client

